Question: Who pays for the remedial actions needed to reach the cross-zonal capacity trajectory, and how must the cost-sharing methodology treat redispatching with cross-border relevance?

Answer: Based on the EU regulatory framework, the answers to your questions are as follows:

**1. Who pays for the remedial actions needed to reach the cross-zonal capacity trajectory?**
The costs are borne by the Member State(s) implementing the action plan. According to **Regulation (EU) 2019/943, Article 15(3)** (consolidated version of 2024-07-16), *"The cost of the remedial actions necessary to achieve the linear trajectory referred to in paragraph 2 or make available cross-zonal capacity at the borders or on critical network elements concerned by the action plan shall be borne by the Member State or Member States implementing the action plan."* Furthermore, Article 15(6) clarifies that if a Member State opts to address remaining internal congestion with remedial actions after the action plan expires, it *"shall cover the costs."*

**2. How must the cost-sharing methodology treat redispatching with cross-border relevance?**
The cost-sharing methodology must explicitly account for and share the costs of cross-border actions among the relevant Transmission System Operators (TSOs). 
* **Inclusion of cross-border solutions:** **Commission Regulation (EU) 2015/1222, Article 74(2)** (consolidated version of 2021-03-15) mandates that *"The redispatching and countertrading cost sharing methodology shall include cost-sharing solutions for actions of cross-border relevance."*
* **Eligibility for regional sharing:** Under **Article 74(4)**, the methodology must determine which costs incurred from using remedial actions (considered in capacity calculations) and which costs incurred to guarantee the firmness of cross-zonal capacity are eligible for sharing among all TSOs within the capacity calculation region.
* **Operational coordination:** **Article 35(2)** of the same regulation requires that the methodology for coordinated redispatching and countertrading *"shall include actions of cross-border relevance and shall enable all TSOs in each capacity calculation region to effectively relieve physical congestion irrespective of whether the reasons for the physical congestion fall mainly outside their control area or not."*

**Sources:**
* [Regulation (EU) 2019/943, Article 15](https://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/02019R0943-20240716)
* [Commission Regulation (EU) 2015/1222, Article 74](https://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/02015R1222-20210315)
* [Commission Regulation (EU) 2015/1222, Article 35](https://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/02015R1222-20210315)

